  "answer": "### Issue Presented\nHow is the minimum corporate income tax (MCIT) computed and when does it apply to a domestic corporation?\n\n### Short Answer\nThe minimum corporate income tax (MCIT) is computed at a rate of 2% of the gross income as of the end of the taxable year, applicable starting from the fourth taxable year following the year in which the corporation commenced its business operations, provided that the MCIT exceeds the regular corporate income tax for that year.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\n- **RR 9-1998**: This regulation provides further details on the implementation of the MCIT.\n\n### Legal Interpretation\n\n### Practical Application\nIn practice, a domestic corporation must assess its gross income at the end of each taxable year to determine if the MCIT applies. If the corporation has been in operation for at least four years and the computed MCIT exceeds the regular corporate income tax, it will be liable to pay the MCIT. Corporations should maintain accurate records of their gross income and tax computations to ensure compliance and to take advantage of any carry-forward provisions for excess MCIT.",
